<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  REV_TrusteeRestricted - column (field) security profile.

  WHY PHASE 1 NEEDS THIS PROFILE AT ALL
  Thirty-nine columns across rev_applicant and rev_application ship with
  IsSecured=1 (2026-08-17: 38 -> 39 - form-field-corrections pass added rev_employmentstatus,
  rev_consentexplanation and rev_intakereviewnote, removed rev_carername and rev_carersupport
  with their columns; net +1). In Dataverse a secured column is readable by NOBODY except a System
  Administrator until a field security profile releases it. Without this profile the
  process owner - who is deliberately NOT a System Administrator (TAD section 6.5,
  ADR-019) - could not read an applicant's own name, and the intake flow could not
  write it. So although the Trustee persona that this profile exists to exclude is a
  Phase 3 concern, the profile itself is a Phase 1 prerequisite, not a Phase 1 option.

  WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT DO
  This file grants Allowed (4) on all three operations for every secured column. The
  control is not in what it grants - it is in who is a MEMBER. Membership is NOT a
  solution component: it is applied per environment by
  provisioning/dataverse/ensure-column-security-profile-members.ps1, which adds the
  group teams REV Admins and REV Service Accounts and nothing else
  (TAD section 6, section 12; NFR-001, NFR-003).

  This is the control that replaces manual anonymisation by a single key holder
  (ADR-002): it is enforced by the platform below the app layer, so no app, view,
  export, API call or flow can bypass it. ADR-002 is Adopted CONDITIONAL on DPO
  sign-off (SDD OQ-004) - see TAD risk A-R21. That gate is above this file.

  REV_FinanceOnly, the second profile in TAD section 6, is NOT built here: it covers
  rev_bankaccount and rev_payment, and neither table exists in Phase 1.

  EVERY IsSecured=1 COLUMN MUST APPEAR BELOW, AND NOTHING ELSE SHOULD.
  A secured column absent from this profile is unreadable even by the process owner, so the
  symptom is not a security hole - it is a blank field nobody can explain, or an intake write
  that fails. The reverse (a permission here for a column that is not secured) is harmless but
  misleading. `scripts/verify-field-security-coverage.py` checks both directions mechanically,
  because this file is the one place in the solution where an omission is silent.

  TWO COLUMNS ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT AND MUST STAY ABSENT: rev_application.rev_breaklocation
  and, since 2026-08-17, rev_application.rev_exceptionalcircumstance. Both are trustee-visible
  by design, on the same rule: rev_breaklocation because a trustee cannot judge a request for a
  break without knowing where the break is (docs/Import/grant-application-data-model-v0.2.md);
  rev_exceptionalcircumstance because a trustee cannot judge a request for EXCEPTIONAL funding
  without knowing the circumstance, extending the same treatment this profile already gives
  rev_conditionprofile and rev_supportrecipientconditionprofile - Article 9 categories that stay
  visible while the free text behind them (rev_otherconditionraw, rev_otherexceptionalcircumstance,
  rev_exceptionalfundingdetail) is secured below. See TAD ADR-002 and ADR-023.

  FILE LOCATION IS NOT A FREE CHOICE - IT IS FIXED BY THE PACKER.
  This file must be Other/FieldSecurityProfiles.xml. SolutionPackagerLib's component
  configuration maps ComponentType.FieldSecurityProfile to directory "Other", file
  "$(type)s.xml", and FieldSecurityProfileProcessor reads that single path and returns
  null - silently, no error - if it is absent. The earlier FieldSecurityProfiles/ folder
  form was therefore dropped from the package without a warning. Likewise the id and
  name are ATTRIBUTES on <FieldSecurityProfile>, not child elements: CreateComponent
  calls Helper.GetAttributeValue(element, "name", throwIfNull: true) and
  Helper.GetAttributeValue(element, "fieldsecurityprofileid", ...). Verified against a
  real `pac solution pack` for both package types - see Dev Summary revision 0.5.

  FIELD-LEVEL SHAPE FIXED 2026-08-14 against a real `pac solution export` of DEV (the
  profile already existed there, created by ensure-schema.ps1 via the Web API, before
  this file's shape was ever validated by import). Two things were wrong and both
  produced no diagnostic beyond "An error occurred while importing Field Security
  Profile.: Object reference not set to an instance of an object." - the importer does
  case-sensitive element lookups with no null check, so a name it doesn't recognise
  reads back null and the next dereference throws:
    - <description> was a CHILD ELEMENT here. It is an ATTRIBUTE on
      <FieldSecurityProfile>, exactly like name and fieldsecurityprofileid above -
      the same convention, just easy to miss because the value is long prose.
    - Every <FieldPermission>'s children were lowercase and, for the attribute
      reference, a different name altogether: entityname/attributelogicalname/
      cancreate/canread/canupdate. The real elements are EntityName/AttributeName/
      CanRead/CanUpdate/CanCreate, in that order, plus a required CanReadUnmasked
      that this file omitted completely (0 for every permission below - none of
      these columns use column-level read-unmasked, only the base CanRead mask).
  <IsCustomizable> and <IntroducedVersion> are also gone from the profile element:
  the DEV export has neither, so they are not part of this component's real shape
  and were removed rather than left as untested guesses.

  fieldsecurityprofileid IS DEV'S REAL, LIVE ID, NOT A FABRICATED ONE. Unlike Role (see
  Roles/*.xml), solution import reconciles an existing FieldSecurityProfile BY NAME even when
  the declared id differs — a live import with the original fabricated id here left DEV's
  profile at its own Web-API-assigned id, silently, rather than erroring. That silent tolerance
  is exactly why the id was still worth fixing here rather than left as a passing landmine:
  nothing currently depends on it, but a future Dataverse version tightening this match (as it
  already does for Role) would turn it into the same hard failure with no warning first.
  Replaced 2026-08-14 with the real id, read live via
  `fieldsecurityprofiles?$filter=name eq 'REV_TrusteeRestricted'`. Same DEV-only caveat as the
  role ids: TST/ACC/PRD get their own id when Pipelines promotes this profile there.
-->
<FieldSecurityProfiles xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <FieldSecurityProfile name="REV_TrusteeRestricted" description="Releases the Tier 4 identifying and special-category columns to the process owner and the service identity only. Every persona that is not a member of this profile - including every trustee - receives no value for these columns from any surface: app, view, export or API." fieldsecurityprofileid="{5fd58153-e997-f111-b8dc-7ced8d43e1b4}">
    <FieldPermissions>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- The applicant's name. ADR-013 keeps the primary name column as a pseudonymised reference precisely so this column is the only place the name lives and can be secured in one move. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_fullname</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- The two columns the name is actually STORED in. rev_fullname above is calculated from these, so securing it while leaving these readable would be security theatre. These are also the two columns the intake flow now matches a repeat applicant on, which is why the service identity needs create, read AND update here. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_firstname</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_lastname</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Form of address. Identity-adjacent, and Mr/Mrs also implies gender, so it sits with the name rather than with the derived reporting columns. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_title</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Direct identifier and the key the intake flow matches a repeat applicant on. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_email</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Direct identifier. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_phone</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Direct identifier. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_addressline</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Direct identifier. Second address line, same treatment as the first. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_addressline2</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Quasi-identifier. A town narrows a person far more than rev_locationarea does, which is the whole reason the region column exists. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_towncity</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Equality-monitoring data. ORDINARY personal data, not Article 9 - see OptionSets/rev_gender.xml. Secured because no eligibility, scoring or trustee decision uses it, so least privilege puts it behind the profile (C-DOM-020). Emily's equality reporting is unaffected: REV Admin is a member. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_gender</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Quasi-identifier. rev_locationarea is derived from it at intake so that nothing downstream needs the postcode itself (FR-027, NFR-013). -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_postcode</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
        <!-- Quasi-identifier. rev_agerange is derived from it at intake for the same reason. -->
        <EntityName>rev_applicant</EntityName>
        <AttributeName>rev_dateofbirth</AttributeName>
        <CanRead>4</CanRead>
        <CanUpdate>4</CanUpdate>
        <CanCreate>4</CanCreate>
        <CanReadUnmasked>0</CanReadUnmasked>
      </FieldPermission>
      <FieldPermission>
